WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:06.420
Running Node.js in Kubernetes responsibly starts before the first manifest is applied.

2
00:00:06.520 --> 00:00:15.560
Build a clean production image with a predictable startup command, repeatable dependency installation, a clear runtime user, and no accidental local files or secrets.

3
00:00:15.660 --> 00:00:19.500
Use Deployments for stateless replicated services when that model fits.

4
00:00:19.600 --> 00:00:28.160
Connect Pods, Services, and Ingress through labels, selectors, ports, and routing rules that are simple enough to review and debug.

5
00:00:28.440 --> 00:00:33.240
Configuration should be explicit and secrets should be protected.

6
00:00:33.340 --> 00:00:40.540
Use ConfigMaps for non-sensitive settings and Secrets or approved secret-delivery mechanisms for sensitive values.

7
00:00:40.640 --> 00:00:47.600
Validate required environment variables at startup and fail clearly when critical values are missing or unsafe.

8
00:00:47.700 --> 00:00:57.060
Avoid logging full environment data, authorization headers, cookies, tokens, private URLs, or configuration objects that contain secrets.

9
00:00:57.160 --> 00:01:01.720
Kubernetes can deliver configuration, but the application must still handle it safely.

10
00:01:02.000 --> 00:01:04.400
Operational behavior is equally important.

11
00:01:04.500 --> 00:01:08.500
Set resource requests and limits based on measurement.

12
00:01:08.600 --> 00:01:13.000
Define readiness, liveness, and startup probes with clear intent.

13
00:01:13.100 --> 00:01:18.220
Handle SIGTERM so rollouts and node maintenance do not drop work unnecessarily.

14
00:01:18.320 --> 00:01:27.200
Harden the workload with non-root execution, limited privilege, narrow service-account permissions, and security context settings that match the service.

15
00:01:27.300 --> 00:01:34.420
Observe production behavior through structured logs, metrics, traces, Kubernetes events, alerts, and runbooks.

16
00:01:34.700 --> 00:01:37.980
A practical first-week improvement plan is straightforward.

17
00:01:38.080 --> 00:01:40.800
Review the Dockerfile and .dockerignore.

18
00:01:40.900 --> 00:01:43.140
Check Deployment and Service selectors.

19
00:01:43.240 --> 00:01:44.680
Add startup validation.

20
00:01:44.780 --> 00:01:46.620
Define or repair probes.

21
00:01:46.720 --> 00:01:50.880
Set initial requests and limits from test or production data.

22
00:01:50.980 --> 00:01:52.900
Verify graceful shutdown.

23
00:01:53.000 --> 00:01:54.760
Restrict service account permissions.

24
00:01:54.860 --> 00:01:57.180
Review how secrets enter the Pod.

25
00:01:57.280 --> 00:02:01.040
Add basic logs and metrics if they are missing.

26
00:02:01.140 --> 00:02:11.540
Finally, make Kubernetes deployment review part of pull requests, CI/CD, release management, and incident response so these controls stay current as the service changes.
